WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Speaker 1: Well, my friends, inflation has&nbsp;
come in and it has come in low. And if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:13.140
want to participate in this ridiculous&nbsp;
game wherein we say, well, if inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:20.520
going up was Joe Biden's fault, then inflation&nbsp;
coming down must be credited to Joe Biden. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:26.760
you and I and many of the people that are in&nbsp;
our audience kind of prefer to operate more in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:33.240
the real world where we don't play these political&nbsp;
games with gas prices and with inflation. So let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.760
not play them. Let's just look at the data and&nbsp;
understand that inflation is a global phenomenon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.120
And we're going to look at global numbers and&nbsp;
we're going to punch the microphone, as I often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.620
do when I get excited. And we'll hopefully have an&nbsp;
understanding of the fact that this is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:52.320
that shouldn't really surprise us because it's&nbsp;
been the most likely trajectory for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.409
although in general we are not particularly&nbsp;
good as humans at making economic predictions.&nbsp;

00:00:57.409 --> 00:01:00.060
Speaker 2: It did seem.
Speaker 1: As though inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.440
peaked some months ago and it would be lower by&nbsp;
the end of the year and indeed it is. So let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:12.000
start with the data. CNBC reporting consumer&nbsp;
prices rose less than expected in November,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.900
up 7.1% from a year ago. The Consumer Price&nbsp;
Index rose just 0.1% from the previous month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:30.180
increased 7.1% from a year ago, compared with&nbsp;
respective estimates of 0.3 and 7.3% core CPI. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:36.840
Consumer Price Index rose 0.2% on the month and&nbsp;
6% on an annual basis, compared with respective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:44.520
estimates of 0.3% and 6.1%. Stocks soared. The&nbsp;
Dow is, as of right now, up about 400 points.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:52.260
Inflation adjusted average hourly earnings for&nbsp;
workers rose 0.5% for the month, though they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:59.820
still down 1.9% from a year ago. Now, whenever we&nbsp;
do these numbers, there is confusion. David, wait.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.640
Are prices up or are they down? What&nbsp;
does it mean? But if prices rose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:13.080
how are they down? Remember that there are two&nbsp;
components to this. What happened last month and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:21.660
how does adding the data from last month change&nbsp;
the last 12 months of inflation? So remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.980
we want a little bit of inflation. We have an&nbsp;
economy. And I know that this gets into. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:34.440
do we really buy into this traditional&nbsp;
economic stuff? And I get that. I get that. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.440
the sort of standard view is you want a little bit&nbsp;
of inflation, a little bit of inflation is good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:46.800
Why? If you have a ton of inflation, things&nbsp;
become unaffordable. So people stop buying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.440
which hurts businesses, so they lay people off&nbsp;
and then those people have no money to buy stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.480
and it's bad for the economy. That's too much&nbsp;
inflation. If you have deflation, it might sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:03.420
good. You might say, sir, ma'am, cheaper stuff is&nbsp;
good. Deflation sounds good. No, it's not good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:10.620
the economy. Why? If you expect prices to continue&nbsp;
declining, if you're looking at a car. Right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.200
an electric vehicle, for example, and you expect&nbsp;
because we're in a deflationary situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.500
that if you wait six months, the prices will be&nbsp;
lower. You wouldn't buy the car. That's bad for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:28.560
the businesses. The employees at the electric car&nbsp;
businesses get laid off because nobody's buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.660
cars because you expect prices to go down. So&nbsp;
the idea is a little bit of inflation creates an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:40.320
incentive to buy now rather than later. It's not&nbsp;
so much that things become unaffordable, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:47.460
is where economic growth comes from now. Believe&nbsp;
me, I'll be the first to tell you those sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:54.960
completely rational actor. Economic analyzes lack&nbsp;
a very real component. I am with you, but those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.240
are the oranges, for lack of a better term, of&nbsp;
why a little bit of inflation is good. Getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:08.040
us back to the numbers. There was a little bit of&nbsp;
inflation last month, but compared to November of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:17.100
21, it was lower. So when we replace in the one&nbsp;
year inflation number, the November 21 number with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:25.860
the November 22 number, year over year, inflation&nbsp;
is lower. Okay. So that's that. Dow surges 400&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:31.620
points as investors cheer the light inflation&nbsp;
report. You know, I don't put a lot of stock in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:38.160
these types of headlines. I think it's important&nbsp;
to remember that, you know, to some degree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:45.480
This has been predicted for months. So if&nbsp;
markets were completely efficient and rational,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.780
this this new data would have already been&nbsp;
priced in. It doesn't really make sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:59.400
the Dow pops because the data was what it is. But&nbsp;
the expectation on paper was of slightly higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:05.580
inflation and it came in lower. Okay. If you're&nbsp;
in the market, it's a good day. And as many of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:11.940
know, much of the return in the markets happens&nbsp;
on just ten days out of the year. Today may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:20.580
one of those days. Lastly, remember that inflation&nbsp;
is a global phenomenon. And when you look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:27.840
inflation rates, these this is data from October&nbsp;
in some countries and November in some you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.120
that among wealthier and larger nations, nations&nbsp;
that are part of this list. We're looking at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.820
trading economics outcome list. You see that the&nbsp;
U.S. is sort of like right in the middle now with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:45.240
year over year inflation at 7.1%, down from the&nbsp;
high eights. And you see that, for example, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:54.000
Italy and in the U.K., inflation is significantly&nbsp;
higher, whereas in Brazil and France and Singapore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:00.660
and Spain, inflation is lower than in the U.S.,&nbsp;
has very little to do with the Joe Biden global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:05.940
phenomenon. U.S. sort of like right in the&nbsp;
middle there. So this is overall, I believe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:13.620
a good thing. Inflation has been too high. It has&nbsp;
been a strain on American budgets and hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:19.560
this continues and we can get down again. We're&nbsp;
just we're not good at this, but it would be nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:25.620
to continue seeing the year over year numbers&nbsp;
tick down over the next six months where maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:32.700
we can get to like the low fives, high fours&nbsp;
by summertime. And that would be historically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:38.220
much more pleasant, I think is the word that I&nbsp;
would use. So that's the latest on inflation.

